{"name":"Down Dog","hex":"#baafb9","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"down dog","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Clipped compound of downward-facing dog."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/down_dog"}],"entries":[{"title":"Downward Dog Pose","blocks":[{"text":"Downward Dog Pose, Downward-facing Dog Pose, or Downdog, also called Adho Mukha Svanasana, is an inversion asana in yoga as exercise. It is often practised as part of a flowing sequence of poses, especially Surya Namaskar, the Salute to the Sun. The asana does not have formally named variations, but several playful variants are used to assist beginning practitioners to become comfortable in the pose."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Downward-Facing-Dog.JPG/330px-Downward-Facing-Dog.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Downward_Dog_Pose"},{"title":"Dog","blocks":[{"text":"The dog is a domesticated descendant of wolves. Also called the domestic dog, it was selectively bred during the Late Pleistocene by hunter-gatherers. Dogs and the modern gray wolf share a common ancestor. Dogs were the first species to be domesticated over 14,000 years ago, before the development of agriculture, though genetic studies suggest the domestication process may have begun over 25,000 years ago. Due to their long association with humans, dogs have gained the ability to thrive on a starch-rich diet that would be inadequate for other canids."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Huskiesatrest.jpg/330px-Huskiesatrest.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dog"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Gajasana_in_Sritattvanidhi.jpg/500px-Gajasana_in_Sritattvanidhi.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Gajasana_in_Sritattvanidhi.jpg/500px-Gajasana_in_Sritattvanidhi.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Gajāsana, Elephant Pose. Hand-drawn illustration in Sritattvanidhi, 19th century Mysore Palace manuscript. The instruction to perform this pose \"over and over again\" in the 18th century Hațhābhyāsapaddhati is suggestive of the repetition of Downward Dog in the Surya Namaskar sequence.[8] ","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGajasana_in_Sritattvanidhi.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d8/Pant_Pratinidhi_1928_Surya_Namaskar_Sequence.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d8/Pant_Pratinidhi_1928_Surya_Namaskar_Sequence.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"Bhawanrao Shriniwasrao Pant Pratinidhi popularised the Sun Salutation in his 1928 book. The sequence uses Downward Dog Pose twice (numbers 4 and 7).[11][12]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APant_Pratinidhi_1928_Surya_Namaskar_Sequence.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Lenovo_YOGA_3_Pro_%2815356226748%29.jpg/500px-Lenovo_YOGA_3_Pro_%2815356226748%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Lenovo_YOGA_3_Pro_%2815356226748%29.jpg/1280px-Lenovo_YOGA_3_Pro_%2815356226748%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Lenovo_YOGA_3_Pro_%2815356226748%29.jpg/500px-Lenovo_YOGA_3_Pro_%2815356226748%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The foldable Lenovo \"YOGΛ\" computer[33]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALenovo_YOGA_3_P